Security Implementation
Security was included in the system because it contains different user accounts and evaluation records. The system is used by students, school heads or principals, and administrators, so each user does not have the same access. Before entering the system, users need to log in using their ID and password.
The system checks the login details entered by the user. If the ID or password is wrong, the user will receive a message that the login information is incorrect. The system also shows how many attempts are left. If the user keeps entering the wrong credentials and uses all the allowed attempts, the user cannot immediately try again. They need to wait for three minutes before they can continue logging in. We included this feature to prevent repeated login attempts.
The features that a user can access also depend on their account role. For example, the administrator is responsible for managing accounts, faculty records, evaluation forms, school years, semesters, and reports. The school head or principal can access the faculty members assigned to them and complete their evaluations. They can also check their evaluation progress and reports. Students mainly use the system to complete the evaluations assigned to them. In this way, users are only able to access the parts of the system that they need.
We also included checking of information entered into the forms. If required information is missing, the system can notify the user before the information is processed. The submitted information goes through the backend and is then stored in the MySQL database. This helps avoid having incomplete records in the system.
Access to evaluation records is also limited depending on the user's role. The system contains faculty evaluation results, ratings, responses, and reports, so these records should only be available to authorized users. These features were included to help make the system safer while still keeping it easy for the users to use.

Development Methodology and Implementation
The development of the system was done little by little. Our group did not make the whole system in one go. We worked on the different parts of the system and checked them as we continued. When we found problems or things that needed to be changed, we went back and improved them before continuing with other features.
We started by looking at the current faculty evaluation process. The school was using Google Forms for evaluations, so we considered some of the difficulties that could happen when there are many responses and records to organize. Based on the problems that we identified, we decided to create a centralized web-based system where the evaluation records and other related information could be managed in one place.
After that, we planned the users and the features of the system. The main users are the administrator, school head or principal, and students. Each of them has different tasks in the system. We also worked on the database, the system processes, the use cases, and the overall structure before and during the development.
For the user interface, we used Next.js, React, TypeScript, Vite, and Tailwind CSS. These were used to build the pages that the users see and interact with. The system includes the login page, dashboards, evaluation forms, faculty information, progress monitoring, and reports.
For the part that handles the system data, we used Node.js and Express.js. The backend receives requests from the frontend and processes the information needed by the system. MySQL is used to store the records, including user accounts, faculty information, evaluation forms, evaluation responses, school years, and semesters.
During development, we used Visual Studio Code to write and manage the code. We also used GitHub to save our progress and keep track of the changes made to the project. EchoAPI was used when testing the API and checking whether the frontend and backend were working properly together. We tested the features while building the system instead of waiting until everything was finished.
At the moment, the system has different functions for each user. The administrator can manage accounts, faculty records, evaluation forms, school years, semesters, and reports. The school head or principal can view their assigned teachers, evaluate them, check their progress, and view the results. Students can log in and answer the evaluations assigned to them.
The report section provides more detailed information about the evaluation results. It can show the faculty member's information, average rating, number of evaluations, responses, rating distribution, results for each question, performance level, and recommendations. The reports can also be printed and exported as PDF or Excel files.
The system is still being checked and improved by the researchers. Some parts may still be adjusted depending on the results of testing. For deployment, our current plan is to use Vercel for the frontend and Railway for the backend and database. However, the system is not yet fully deployed, so this is still part of our planned implementation.

